Development of reusable Stack and Queue implementations using Python type hints and dataclasses.
# Experiment 03 – Stack and Queue

## Aim
Develop a reusable Python package implementing Stack and Queue using type hints and dataclasses.

## Algorithm / Procedure
1. Create a Stack class using a dataclass.
2. Implement push and pop operations.
3. Create a Queue class using a dataclass.
4. Implement enqueue and dequeue operations.
5. Test both data structures.

## Program
from dataclasses import dataclass
from typing import List


@dataclass
class Stack:
    items: List[int]

    def push(self, item: int) -> None:
        self.items.append(item)

    def pop(self) -> int:
        return self.items.pop()


@dataclass
class Queue:
    items: List[int]

    def enqueue(self, item: int) -> None:
        self.items.append(item)

    def dequeue(self) -> int:
        return self.items.pop(0)


# Stack
stack = Stack([])

stack.push(10)
stack.push(20)
stack.push(30)

print("Stack:", stack.items)
print("Popped:", stack.pop())
print("Stack after pop:", stack.items)


# Queue
queue = Queue([])

queue.enqueue(40)
queue.enqueue(50)
queue.enqueue(60)

print("\nQueue:", queue.items)
print("Dequeued:", queue.dequeue())
print("Queue after dequeue:", queue.items)

## Output
Stack: [10, 20, 30]
Popped: 30
Stack after pop: [10, 20]

Queue: [40, 50, 60]
Dequeued: 40
Queue after dequeue: [50, 60]

## Result
Stack and Queue were successfully implemented using Python dataclasses and type hints.
